findings.body:
The new `RolloutsSettingMessage` translator comment cites `%1$@`, but the localized value uses a plain `%@`:

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Shared/Strings.swift" lines="6257-6261"}

::callout{type="warning"}
`moz-xliff-lint` extracts placeables from the value with `%(?:\d+\$@|@|d)` and requires every one of them to appear in the comment. Here the value's set is `{%@}` while the comment's set is `{%1$@}`, so the `Firefox: reference string linter` workflow reports "Comment does not include the following placeables: %@" and exits non-zero. The sibling `StudiesSettingMessageV3` comment already gets this right.

Match the value exactly:

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/Shared/Strings.swift" lines="6261-6261"}
        comment: "A short description that explains that Mozilla may change features remotely. %@ is the app name (e.g. Firefox).")

`formatStudiesText` formats `strings.detailTextStudies` — the old v136 copy, "%@ may install and run studies from time to time." — instead of the `detailTextStudiesV2` constant added on line 207. Once #30988 wires this helper into the split Studies toggle, users will see the old description while the new v148 copy this PR exists to add sits unused. `formatRolloutsText` correctly uses the new `detailTextRollouts`, which makes the mismatch easy to overlook.

::diff{file="focus-ios/Blockzilla/UIComponents/UIConstants.swift" lines="417-430"}

::suggestion{file="focus-ios/Blockzilla/UIComponents/UIConstants.swift" lines="423-425"}
        func formatStudiesText(appName: String) -> String {
            return String(format: strings.detailTextStudiesV2, appName)
        }

The six Firefox keys follow the repo's existing conventions: versioned key names, `%@` app-name placeholders instead of hard-coded brands, and translator comments that name the placeholder. The en-CA/en-GB `Settings.strings` tables were kept in sync, and the Rollouts structure mirrors Studies cleanly. The new Focus constants and `SettingsTextFormatter` are otherwise unreferenced, consistent with the PR's stated intent to land copy ahead of #30988.

One thing to confirm: `detailTextSendUsageDataV2` (line 203) duplicates the exact English text of `detailTextSendUsageData` under a new key and isn't referenced anywhere in this PR. A new key means all Focus locales lose their existing translation of that sentence until the l10n cycle catches up, so if #30988 doesn't require it, reusing the original constant avoids that churn.

::code{file="focus-ios/Blockzilla/UIComponents/UIConstants.swift" lines="202-209"}
